-- title:
--   Kernel of the uniformiser has degree q²
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $k$ a field, and write $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2} =$ `Zp2 q` for the Witt vectors of the field $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ (`GaloisField q 2`). Fix a ring homomorphism $j_0 \colon \mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \to k$ and let $X_0$ be a special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $k$ relative to $j_0$, that is: a two-dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ over $k$, a family of series $\mathrm{act}(a)$ indexed by $a \in \mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ and a further series $\varpi = (\varpi_1,\varpi_2)$ in two variables, all endomorphisms of $F$ as formal group laws, with $\mathrm{act}$ a ring action (sending $1$ to the identity series, products to composites and sums to $F$-sums) and with $\varpi \circ \varpi = \mathrm{act}(q)$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ for the Witt vector Frobenius $\sigma$; subject moreover to speciality at $j_0$ (the two Lie summands `lieZero` and `lieOne` are complementary and each invertible as a $k$-module) and to height $4$, i.e. the predicate `HasKernelOfDegree` holds for the series $\mathrm{act}(q)$ with value $q^4$. The conclusion is that the quotient of $k[\![X_1,X_2]\!]$ by the ideal generated by the two components $\varpi_1, \varpi_2$ is a finite-dimensional $k$-module, of dimension exactly $q^2$.
--
--   The quotient in question is the coordinate ring of the kernel $X_0[\varpi]$, so the statement says that the uniformiser of a special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module of height $4$ is an isogeny of degree $q^2$ (height $2$), as in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation literature. It is used downstream in the analysis of the ideal generated by $\varpi$, in particular in the results identifying membership in that ideal and the substitution description of it.
